            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Organizing Code with Methods</h2>
                    <p>As your programs grow, you'll want to organize code into reusable blocks called <strong>methods</strong> (also known as functions). Methods help you avoid repetition, make code easier to read, and simplify debugging.</p>
                    
                    <p>In this lesson, you'll learn how to create methods, pass data to them, and get results back.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>What is a Method?</h2>
                    <p>A <strong>method</strong> is a named block of code that performs a specific task. You've already been using methods like <code>Console.WriteLine()</code> and <code>Console.ReadLine()</code>.</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// You've been calling methods all along!
Console.WriteLine("Hello");     // WriteLine is a method
string input = Console.ReadLine();  // ReadLine is a method
int length = input.Length;      // Length is a property (similar concept)</code></pre>

                    <h3>Why Use Methods?</h3>
                    <div class="benefits-grid">
                        <div class="benefit-item">
                            <span class="benefit-icon">🔄</span>
                            <h4>Reusability</h4>
                            <p>Write code once, use it many times</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="benefit-item">
                            <span class="benefit-icon">📦</span>
                            <h4>Organization</h4>
                            <p>Break complex problems into smaller pieces</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="benefit-item">
                            <span class="benefit-icon">🔧</span>
                            <h4>Maintainability</h4>
                            <p>Fix bugs in one place instead of many</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="benefit-item">
                            <span class="benefit-icon">📖</span>
                            <h4>Readability</h4>
                            <p>Give meaningful names to blocks of code</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Creating Your First Method</h2>
                    <p>Here's the basic syntax for defining a method:</p>

                    <div class="syntax-box">
                        <code>returnType MethodName(parameters) { // code }</code>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// A simple method that prints a greeting
void SayHello()
{
    Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");
}

// Calling the method
SayHello();  // Output: Hello, World!
SayHello();  // Output: Hello, World!
SayHello();  // Output: Hello, World!</code></pre>

                    <h3>Method Components</h3>
                    <div class="method-anatomy">
                        <div class="anatomy-item">
                            <code>void</code>
                            <span>Return type – <code>void</code> means the method doesn't return a value</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-item">
                            <code>SayHello</code>
                            <span>Method name – use PascalCase (capitalize each word)</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-item">
                            <code>()</code>
                            <span>Parameters – inputs to the method (empty here)</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-item">
                            <code>{ }</code>
                            <span>Method body – the code that runs when called</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Parameters and Arguments</h2>
                    <p>Parameters let you pass data into a method. Arguments are the actual values you provide when calling the method.</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Method with one parameter
void Greet(string name)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Hello, {name}!");
}

// Calling with arguments
Greet("Alice");   // Output: Hello, Alice!
Greet("Bob");     // Output: Hello, Bob!

// Method with multiple parameters
void DisplayInfo(string name, int age)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{name} is {age} years old.");
}

DisplayInfo("Alice", 25);  // Output: Alice is 25 years old.
DisplayInfo("Bob", 30);    // Output: Bob is 30 years old.</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Parameters vs Arguments</span>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Parameters</strong> are the variables in the method definition. <strong>Arguments</strong> are the values you pass when calling the method.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Return Values</h2>
                    <p>Methods can return a value back to the caller using the <code>return</code> keyword:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Method that returns an int
int Add(int a, int b)
{
    return a + b;
}

// Using the returned value
int sum = Add(5, 3);
Console.WriteLine(sum);  // Output: 8

// Can use directly in expressions
Console.WriteLine(Add(10, 20));  // Output: 30
int result = Add(Add(1, 2), Add(3, 4));  // Nested calls: 3 + 7 = 10</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Method that returns a string
string GetGreeting(string name)
{
    return $"Hello, {name}!";
}

string message = GetGreeting("Alice");
Console.WriteLine(message);  // Output: Hello, Alice!

// Method that returns a bool
bool IsAdult(int age)
{
    return age &gt;= 18;
}

if (IsAdult(25))
{
    Console.WriteLine("You can vote!");
}</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box warning">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Return Ends Execution</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>When a <code>return</code> statement executes, the method ends immediately. Any code after <code>return</code> won't run.</p>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">string GetStatus(int score)
{
    if (score &gt;= 50)
    {
        return "Pass";  // Method exits here if true
    }
    return "Fail";  // Only runs if condition was false
}

// Multiple return points
string GetGrade(int score)
{
    if (score &gt;= 90) return "A";
    if (score &gt;= 80) return "B";
    if (score &gt;= 70) return "C";
    if (score &gt;= 60) return "D";
    return "F";
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Optional Parameters</h2>
                    <p>You can give parameters default values, making them optional:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Parameter with default value
void Greet(string name, string greeting = "Hello")
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{greeting}, {name}!");
}

Greet("Alice");              // Output: Hello, Alice!
Greet("Bob", "Hi");          // Output: Hi, Bob!
Greet("Charlie", "Welcome"); // Output: Welcome, Charlie!

// Multiple optional parameters
void CreateUser(string name, int age = 0, string country = "Unknown")
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Name: {name}, Age: {age}, Country: {country}");
}

CreateUser("Alice");                    // Uses both defaults
CreateUser("Bob", 25);                  // Uses country default
CreateUser("Charlie", 30, "USA");       // No defaults used</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Rule</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Optional parameters must come after required parameters in the method signature.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Named Arguments</h2>
                    <p>You can specify arguments by name, making code clearer and allowing you to skip optional parameters:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">void SendEmail(string to, string subject, string body, bool urgent = false)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"To: {to}");
    Console.WriteLine($"Subject: {subject}");
    Console.WriteLine($"Body: {body}");
    Console.WriteLine($"Urgent: {urgent}");
}

// Using named arguments
SendEmail(
    to: "alice@example.com",
    subject: "Meeting",
    body: "See you at 3pm",
    urgent: true
);

// Skip optional parameter, specify later one
SendEmail(
    to: "bob@example.com",
    subject: "Hello",
    body: "Just saying hi"
);

// Mix positional and named (positional must come first)
SendEmail("charlie@example.com", "Test", body: "Testing", urgent: false);</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Method Overloading</h2>
                    <p>You can have multiple methods with the same name but different parameters. This is called <strong>overloading</strong>:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Same name, different parameters
int Add(int a, int b)
{
    return a + b;
}

int Add(int a, int b, int c)
{
    return a + b + c;
}

double Add(double a, double b)
{
    return a + b;
}

// C# picks the right one based on arguments
Console.WriteLine(Add(1, 2));        // Calls int Add(int, int) → 3
Console.WriteLine(Add(1, 2, 3));     // Calls int Add(int, int, int) → 6
Console.WriteLine(Add(1.5, 2.5));    // Calls double Add(double, double) → 4.0</code></pre>

                    <p>Real-world example:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">void Log(string message)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"[INFO] {message}");
}

void Log(string message, string level)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"[{level.ToUpper()}] {message}");
}

void Log(string message, string level, DateTime timestamp)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"[{timestamp:HH:mm:ss}] [{level.ToUpper()}] {message}");
}

Log("Application started");
Log("Connection failed", "error");
Log("User logged in", "info", DateTime.Now);</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Expression-Bodied Methods</h2>
                    <p>For simple methods, you can use the <code>=&gt;</code> syntax for a more concise form:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Traditional method
int Multiply(int a, int b)
{
    return a * b;
}

// Expression-bodied method (same thing, shorter)
int Multiply(int a, int b) =&gt; a * b;

// More examples
bool IsEven(int number) =&gt; number % 2 == 0;
string GetFullName(string first, string last) =&gt; $"{first} {last}";
void SayHi(string name) =&gt; Console.WriteLine($"Hi, {name}!");

// Using them
Console.WriteLine(Multiply(4, 5));     // 20
Console.WriteLine(IsEven(10));         // True
Console.WriteLine(GetFullName("John", "Doe"));  // John Doe</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>ref and out Parameters</h2>
                    <p>Normally, parameters are passed by value (a copy is made). Use <code>ref</code> and <code>out</code> to pass by reference:</p>

                    <h3>ref – Pass by Reference</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">void Double(ref int number)
{
    number = number * 2;  // Modifies the original variable
}

int value = 5;
Double(ref value);
Console.WriteLine(value);  // Output: 10 (not 5!)

// Swap example
void Swap(ref int a, ref int b)
{
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}

int x = 1, y = 2;
Swap(ref x, ref y);
Console.WriteLine($"x={x}, y={y}");  // x=2, y=1</code></pre>

                    <h3>out – Return Multiple Values</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// out parameters must be assigned before the method returns
void GetMinMax(int[] numbers, out int min, out int max)
{
    min = numbers.Min();
    max = numbers.Max();
}

int[] data = { 5, 2, 9, 1, 7 };
GetMinMax(data, out int minimum, out int maximum);
Console.WriteLine($"Min: {minimum}, Max: {maximum}");  // Min: 1, Max: 9

// You've seen this pattern with TryParse!
if (int.TryParse("123", out int result))
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Parsed: {result}");
}

// Discard out parameters you don't need with _
if (int.TryParse("456", out _))
{
    Console.WriteLine("It's a valid number");
}</code></pre>

                    <div class="comparison-box">
                        <div class="comparison-item">
                            <h4>ref</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Variable must be initialized before</li>
                                <li>Method can read and modify</li>
                                <li>Use to modify an existing value</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-item">
                            <h4>out</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Variable doesn't need initialization</li>
                                <li>Method must assign a value</li>
                                <li>Use to return multiple values</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Local Functions</h2>
                    <p>You can define methods inside other methods. These are called <strong>local functions</strong>:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">void ProcessData(int[] numbers)
{
    // Local function - only accessible within ProcessData
    int CalculateSum()
    {
        int total = 0;
        foreach (int n in numbers)
        {
            total += n;
        }
        return total;
    }

    double CalculateAverage()
    {
        return (double)CalculateSum() / numbers.Length;
    }

    Console.WriteLine($"Sum: {CalculateSum()}");
    Console.WriteLine($"Average: {CalculateAverage():F2}");
}

int[] data = { 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 };
ProcessData(data);
// Output:
// Sum: 150
// Average: 30.00</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Methods are reusable blocks of code that perform specific tasks.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Parameters let you pass data in; return values let you get data out.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Optional parameters have default values; named arguments improve clarity.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Method overloading allows multiple methods with the same name but different parameters.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>ref</code> to modify values and <code>out</code> to return multiple values.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>

